1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Find eleven in this sorted list. Binary search checks
the middle, rather than trying every number.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
The middle is eight. Eleven is larger.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Because the list is sorted, eight and everything to its
left are too small. Seven candidates remain.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Now check twelve, the middle of the remaining seven.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Eleven is smaller, so discard twelve and everything
to its right. Three remain.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Check ten. Eleven is larger, leaving just eleven.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Check it: found. The candidate counts went
fifteen, seven, three, one.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Each unsuccessful comparison removes the middle and
one side, leaving at most half the candidates.

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Sorted order makes that safe. Without it,
discarded values could include our target.
